Traditionally in France, voting is low-tech: voters are isolated in a booth, indicate their chosen candidate on a pre-printed sheet of paper and put it in an envelope.
Once officials have confirmed the identity of the voter, he or she drops the envelope on the ballot box and signs the electoral list.
France's election law quite strictly sets out the procedures.
As of 1988, the ballot boxes should be transparent so that voters and observers can see that there are no envelopes at the beginning of the vote inside and that no envelopes other than those of the duly numbered and eligible voters are released.
Candidates can send representatives to see each part of the process. In the evening, volunteers count the votes under strict supervision by following specific procedures.
