When a small group of living things (small population) is separated from the main population from which it originates (for example, if it passes through a mountain range or river, or if it moves to a new island so that the return is difficult), it will often end up in a different environment than before.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population will need different functions or adaptations in order to be a strong competitor.
Indigenous people have not changed at all, it still needs the same adaptation as before.
Over time, when the new population began to adapt to its new environment, it began to look less like the rest of the population.
After all, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will differ so much that they won't be able to be called the same species.
This process is called species formation, which means the formation of new species. Species formation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
