Taiwan originated in the 15th century, when European sailors passing by recorded the name of the island as “Ilha Formosa” or “beautiful island”.
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in southwestern Taiwan by introducing changes to local grain-producing customs and employing Chinese workers to labor in its rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, the troops of the Qing Dynasty (1644-1912) took control of the northern and western coastal regions of Taiwan, and in 1885 declared Taiwan a province of the Qing Empire.
In 1895, after the loss of the First Chinese-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Shimonoseki Treaty, giving Japan sovereignty over Taiwan, which ruled the island until 1945.
