The true Middle Ages was preceded by the early and followed by the late, which, according to the convention, ended around 1500.
Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a wide range of ideas in practice - from the technological impetus or the technological imperative to the strict feeling that human destiny is guided by logic related to scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Two common ideas are shared between most interpretations of technological determinism: the very development of technology goes down a path far beyond cultural or political influence, or technology itself influences inherent rather than socially conditioned societies.
So, for example, one can say that a motor vehicle necessarily leads to the development of roads.
However, a national road network will not be economically advantageous only for a handful of cars, so new methods of production are being developed to reduce the value of owning a car.
The mass ownership of cars has also led to the greater incidence of road accidents, necessitating the invention of new healthcare techniques to restore damaged bodies.
Romanticism contains a large element of cultural determinism, the beginning of which was set by writers such as Goethe, Fichte and Schlegel.
In the context of romanticism, geography shapes individuals, and over time, geography-related customs and culture arise, and they, being in harmony with the place of society, were better than arbitrarily imposed laws.
In the way Paris is known as the fashion capital of the modern world, Constantinople is considered the fashion capital of feudal Europe.
Its fame as an epicenter of luxury dates back to about 400 B.C. and continues until about 1100 B.C.
Its status declined in the twelfth century mainly due to the fact that the Crusaders returned with gifts such as spices and silk, which were more appreciated than those offered by the Byzantine markets.
At that time, the title “Fashion Capital” was transferred from Constantinople to Paris.
Gothic style has peaked in the period between the 10–11th centuries and the 14th century.
