At the end of the Middle Ages, Western Europe began to develop its own style. As a result of the crusades, people began to use buttons to fasten their clothes, which is one of the greatest developments of this time.
Agriculture as a means of subsistence is such agriculture, which is carried out for the production of sufficient food only for the needs of the farmer and his family.
Market agriculture is a simple, often organic system, often using preserved seeds from the same ecological region, combined with crop rotation and other relatively simple techniques for maximum yield.
Historically, most farmers have engaged in subsistence farming, and this still applies to many developing countries.
Subcultures unite like-minded people who feel neglected by societal standards. They allow them to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures may differ in age, ethnicity, class, location and/or gender of members.
Signs that define a subculture as independent can be of linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical nature or a combination of factors.
Members of a subculture often signal their membership through the characteristic and symbolic use of style, which includes fashion, manners and slang.
One of the most commonly used means of illustrating the importance of socialization is the example of several unfortunate cases of children who, due to neglect, mistreatment or intentional violence, have not been socialized by adults in the period of children's growth.
These children are called “untamed” or wild. Some untamed children have been kept imprisoned by people (usually their own parents); sometimes the abandonment of such children was due to the rejection of severe intellectual or physical harm to the child by the parents.
Wild children before being abandoned or escaped may have been gravely abused or traumatized.
Others are presumed to have been raised by animals; for some they say they lived alone in the wild.
When fully brought from animals, the wild child demonstrates behavior (within physical limitations) almost entirely resembling that of the particular caring animal, as well as his fear or indifference to humans.
