Studies in the twentieth century have shown that there are 2 groups of genetic variations: hidden and pronounced.
The mutation adds a new genetic variation, and the selection removes it from the group of pronounced variations.
Segregation and recombination stir variations forward - backward between the two pools with each generation.
In savannah, it's hard for a digestive system like that of people to satisfy their need for amino acids through available plant resources.
What's more, the consequences of failure are serious: growth depression, malnutrition and, ultimately, death.
The most readily available plant resources would be proteins from the leaves and seeds of legumes, but they are difficult to digest by us unless cooked.
In contrast, foods of animal origin (termites, ants, eggs) are not only easily digestible, but also provide a large amount of protein, which contains all the essential amino acids.
Considering all this, we should not be surprised if our own ancestors solved their “protein problem” in the same way that the chimpanzees in the savannah do today.
Interruption of sleep is the process of intentional awakening during your usual sleep time and falling asleep a little later (10-60 minutes).
This can be done easily with a relatively quiet alarm clock to bring you back to consciousness, without waking you up completely.
If you find yourself turning off the clock in your dream, you can place it at the other end of the room, thus forcing yourself to get out of bed to turn it off.
Other biorhythm-based options include drinking plenty of fluids (especially water or tea known as a diuretic) before bed, forcing a person to get up to urinate.
