Railways were built in England as early as the 16th century.
Although railroads consisted simply of parallel wooden boards, they allowed the horses to achieve greater speed and pull larger loads than on the more unequal roads typical of that time.
Traverses were introduced relatively early to keep the rails in place. Gradually, however, it became clear that the rails would be more efficient with an iron lane from above.
This became a common practice, but the iron caused more wear on the wooden wheels of the wagons.
Gradually, the wooden wheels were replaced with iron wheels. In 1767, the first all-iron rails were introduced.
