After being passed by Congress on July 4 and signed by the President of Congress, John Hancock, and the Secretary, Charles Thomson, the handwritten copy was sent to John Dunlap's printing house, located a few blocks away.
Between 150 and 200 copies were made at night, now known as “Dunlap broadsides.”
The document was first read publicly by John Nixo in the courtyard of Independence Hall on July 8.
One was sent to George Washington on July 6, who read it to his soldiers on July 9 in New York. A copy arrives in London on August 10.
Dunlap’s 25 one-sided copies of large sheets that are still known to exist are the oldest surviving copies of the document. The original manuscript has not survived.
