They often have more autonomy than regular team members, as their teams can meet according to different time zones, which their local leadership may not understand.
The presence of a truly “invisible team” (Larsson and Lafasto, 1989, p.109), is also a unique component of the virtual team.
The “invisible team” is the management team to which each of the members reports. This team sets the standards for each member.
