The modern city of Casablanca was founded in the 10th century BC by Berber fishermen and was used by Phoenicians, Romans and Marinides as a strategic port called Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and rebuilt it under the name “Casa Branca”, but left it after an earthquake in 1755.
The Moroccan sultan restored the city as Dar el-Beida, and the name Casablanca he received from Spanish merchants who set up their commercial bases there.
Throughout Morocco, Casablanca is one of the least interesting places for shopping.
Easy to find places around the old Medina, where traditional Moroccan goods are sold, such as tagins, ceramics, leather goods, hookahs and a whole spectrum of trinkets, but it's all for tourists.
Goma is a tourist town in the Democratic Republic of Congo in the far east to Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by lava from the Niragongo volcano, which flooded most of the city's streets and especially downtown.
While Goma is relatively safe, all visits outside Goma must be explored to understand the state of the fighting that continues in the North Kivu province.
The city is also the base for climbing the Niragongo volcano, along with some of the cheapest ways to trace mountain gorillas in Africa.
You can use “boda-boda” (motorcycle-taxi) to move around Goma. The normal (local) price is ~500 Congolese francs for a short trip.
