Sundarban is the largest coastal mangrove belt in the world, which extends 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coast of Bangladesh and India.
Sundarban has been declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. This part of the forest, which is located on Indian territory, is called the Sundarban National Park.
The forests, however, are not only made of mangrove swamps — they include some of the last remnants of the great jungles that once engulfed the Ganges plain.
Sundarban occupies an area of 3850 km2, and almost a third of this territory is covered with water and swamps.
Since 1966, Sundarbaan has been a wildlife reserve, and it is believed that there are already 400 royal Bengal tigers in the area and about 30,000 spotted deer.
