The Japanese culture of work is more hierarchical and official than what Westerners may be accustomed to.
Costumes are standard business attire, and colleagues turn to each other with last names or positions.
Harmony in the workplace is extremely important, emphasizing group efforts, not promoting individual achievements.
Workers often have to obtain the approval of superiors for each of their decisions and are expected to follow their instructions unquestioningly.
